whose Yoshi alright that's a dumb  question we all know that he's Mario's  magical dinosaur friend but defining  Yoshi's new island is a bit trickier yes  it's a sequel to the beloved Super  Nintendo original but what is it outside  of that relationship  honestly it's impossible to separate  those two games Yoshi's new game is so  similar to his old game that it splashes  around in those 20-year old memories  without forging an identity of its own  that's not to say it's not pleasant game  because it is a Yoshi's new island is  also quite forgettable Yoshi is a  slowest dinosaur around he can't even  run now we know why his kind went  extinct his lack of fast locomotion  dictates the pace of this platformer the  crazy dexterous challenges from its many  peers are nowhere to be found here  instead exploration is the key sure you  may be able to make it to the end of  each stage without any problems but can  you find every smiling flower the sense  of discovery is highlighted by branching  level design walls it appears solid  reveal their true nature when you come  close to them so be sure to poke your  nose in every corner much of the appeal  comes from exploring the twisty and  colorful levels the happy atmosphere is  so infectious you can't help but smile  Yoshi's new island is just a nice place  to spend some time  Yoshi isn't that mobile but he can't  hover and if I mentioned his tongue it's  kind of gross but very useful you can  gobble up just about any enemy if your  intestines are up to the task  poop out a proper a Yoshi is a fine egg  tosser stun enemies and reveal secrets  by throwing unhatched Yoshi's  willy-nilly and if you run out there are  usually more eggs nearby so you don't  have to aim that wall to progress and  there's really not much challenge  elsewhere either that's great for people  who would rather reach the end without  any real obstacles but it does make for  a ho-hum adventure sleepwalking to  victory just gets tiring after a while  even though you can replay levels to  find every hidden item there's not much  motivation to do so there's a little  variety that don't expect many thrills  from the detours either for instance  when you jump into psychedelic doors  Yoshi transforms into a sentient vehicle  tilt the 3ds to maneuver the jackhammer  or submarine through tight tunnels and  nab as many coins as you can these  sections provide a nice enough change of  pace but stiff controls eliminate any  potential join these mechanical  transformations sure Yoshi's new island  is easy but that's not why it's so bland  plenty of easy games have been great in  the past after all so why does this  suffer while Kirby's Epic Yarn excelled  it's all in the imagination more  specifically Yoshi's new island is  really unimaginable ooh the most engine  each level because there aren't any  interesting obstacles blocking your path  and the many collectables don't add much  either once you learned that those  floating age clouds hide an  out-of-the-way corners you're halfway to  completing the game so this is an easy  game with few surprises pretty ho-hum  experience the East seeps through the  health system as well and that's not a  good thing just like in the original  Yoshi's Island you carry Mario on your  back and he gets knocked off of an enemy  touches you you then have a few seconds  again and back but also you lose a life  it's a clever concept but one that  creates annoying encounters avoiding  enemies isn't a problem but if you do  get hit you have to suffer through  Mario's awful cries while dumbly chasing  after him the system doesn't make the  game any harder just interrupts the  already tedious piecing the health  system is at its worst during boss  battles here you need to put some  thought in how to hurt your mean  opponent once you figure out the trick  you don't need to do much to win  let the boss slam into you once you're  hit just scoop Mario back up and toss an  egg at the big baddie because of how a  health works the bosses pose little  threat there just another minor  roadblock on your way to the end credits  Yoshi's new island is certainly a  pleasant adventure it's colorful and  welcoming and even though its best  elements first surfaced more than 20  years ago it's still fun to explore the  branching worlds but the game lacks  a real identity and never presents  interesting challenges Yoshi's new  island works as an introductory  platformer but otherwise lacks the  excitement that would make for a  gripping adventure
 

